---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="Methane Outliers" path="/">
  <header class="masthead">
    <div>
      <h1>Who flares and vents the most, relative to production?</h1>
      <p class="tagline">
        The top 50 reporting units in Alberta and Texas, ranked by
        <strong>flared+vented gas as a share of gas-equivalent
        production</strong> over the same rolling 12-month window.
      </p>
    </div>
    <span class="window-badge" id="window-badge" hidden></span>
  </header>

  <section class="intro">
    <p>
      Operators burn methane for the same reason people throw away
      packing peanuts: they wanted the thing in the box, not the
      filler. In West Texas the box is oil, the filler is associated
      gas &mdash; and gas needs a pipeline while oil just needs a
      truck. When drilling outruns midstream, or Waha prices go
      negative and buyers effectively charge you to take it, the
      arithmetic is brutal and boring: burn the cheap byproduct, keep
      selling the expensive one. Add completions flowback, plant
      turnarounds and the occasional Texas freeze, and a flare is
      really just the relief valve for a midstream system&rsquo;s bad
      day.
    </p>
    <p>
      Alberta solved the nowhere-to-put-it problem decades ago and got
      stuck with the subtler one: gas that leaks by design.
      Pneumatic controllers running on produced gas, tank vapors,
      dehydrator stills, heavy-oil casing gas too thin and remote to
      be worth a compressor &mdash; all quietly exhaled rather than
      dramatically burned. That distinction matters, because venting
      is 100% methane and flaring is roughly 98% destruction &mdash;
      except <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abq0385">the
      surveys keep finding about one flare in ten unlit or
      sulking</a>, which is how a solution becomes a rounding error.
      It is also why the maps below count the two together. The last
      decade&rsquo;s real progress came less from regulators than from
      investors, LNG buyers and satellites that could finally
      <a href="https://carbonmapper.org">count the flames from
      orbit</a> &mdash; and the rules, in both jurisdictions, have
      spent 2025 and 2026 quietly loosening again.
    </p>
    <p class="scale-note">
      For scale: 1 MMcf &mdash; the unit in the tables below &mdash;
      is about a year&rsquo;s natural gas for a dozen homes, or the
      energy in ~9,000 gallons of gasoline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="controls">
    <div class="slider-wrap">
      <label for="window-slider" id="slider-label">12 months ending &hellip;</label>
      <input type="range" id="window-slider" min="0" max="0" value="0" />
    </div>
    <label class="toggle">
      <input type="checkbox" id="show-all" />
      show all units <span class="muted">(latest window)</span>
    </label>
    <div class="legend" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="legend-title">Flared+vented / production</span>
      <span class="swatch" style="--c: var(--seq-150)"></span><span>&lt;0.1%</span>
      <span class="swatch" style="--c: var(--seq-300)"></span><span>0.1&ndash;1%</span>
      <span class="swatch" style="--c: var(--seq-450)"></span><span>1&ndash;5%</span>
      <span class="swatch" style="--c: var(--seq-550)"></span><span>5&ndash;20%</span>
      <span class="swatch ring" style="--c: var(--seq-700)"></span><span>&gt;20% &middot; ring = top 50</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <main class="duo">
    <section class="jur">
      <div class="jur-head">
        <h2 id="ab-head">Alberta <span class="muted">facilities</span></h2>
        <div class="search-row">
          <span class="search-count" id="ab-count"></span>
          <input type="search" id="ab-search" autocomplete="off"
            placeholder="Search facility or operator (e.g. cenovus)&hellip;" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="ab-map" class="map-box" role="application"
        aria-label="Alberta top emitters"></div>
      <div class="table-card">
      <table class="outliers" id="ab-list">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Facility</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">MMcf</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">%</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody></tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="jur">
      <div class="jur-head">
        <h2 id="tx-head">Texas <span class="muted">leases</span></h2>
        <div class="search-row">
          <span class="search-count" id="tx-count"></span>
          <input type="search" id="tx-search" autocomplete="off"
            placeholder="Search lease or operator (e.g. apache)&hellip;" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="tx-map" class="map-box" role="application"
        aria-label="Texas top emitters"></div>
      <div class="table-card">
      <table class="outliers" id="tx-list">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Lease</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">MMcf</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">%</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody></tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <section class="story">
    <h2>Same lens, different regimes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The measure.</strong> Flared+vented gas divided by
        gas-equivalent production (gas + liquids at energy
        equivalence), summed over a 12-month window &mdash; a unitless
        ratio computed identically on both sides (Alberta volumes
        converted at 1 e3m&sup3; = 35.3147 MCF). A unit needs
        &ge;2,000 MCF flared+vented and real production in the window
        to be ranked; dot size is flared+vented volume.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The units.</strong> Alberta reports against facilities
        (batteries, plants); Texas against leases. Both are the
        operator&rsquo;s reporting unit above the well &mdash; neither
        jurisdiction publishes complete well-level attribution, so
        this is as close as public data gets.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The windows align exactly.</strong> The slider steps
        through every 12-month window both public archives share.
        Alberta&rsquo;s archive is a rolling ~5 years; Texas publishes
        back to 1993 but its two newest months are still filling in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Transparency differs.</strong> Alberta publishes vented
        and flared volumes separately for every facility. Texas
        operators have reported the split since 2021, but every bulk
        product the RRC publishes folds it back into one
        &ldquo;vented or flared&rdquo; number &mdash; so the combined
        series is what both maps show.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not a scoreboard.</strong> Different rules, different
        measurement, and both jurisdictions under-report: satellite
        and aerial campaigns routinely observe more flaring and
        venting than official filings admit &mdash; see
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abq0385">Plant et
        al. 2022 (<em>Science</em>)</a> on unlit and inefficient
        flares, and the
        <a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/global-methane-tracker-2025">IEA
        Global Methane Tracker</a> on the gap between reported and
        observed emissions. Absolute levels aren&rsquo;t comparable;
        the ratio, the outlier structure, and the trends are.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
      Data: <a href="https://www.petrinex.ca">Petrinex</a> (Alberta,
      refreshed weekly) and the Railroad Commission of Texas PDQ dump
      plus public GIS well locations (monthly). Alberta facility
      locations are DLS centroids (median ~270 m); Texas leases sit at
      the median surface location of their wells. Contains information
      licensed from Petrinex; data &copy; Government of Alberta.
      Non-commercial demonstration; derived statistics only.
    </p>
  </section>

  <script src="../scripts/main.js"></script>
</Base>

<style>
  .slider-wrap {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 250px;
    max-width: 520px;
  }
  .slider-wrap label {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .slider-wrap input[type="range"] {
    flex: 1;
    accent-color: var(--accent);
  }
  .toggle {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .toggle input { accent-color: var(--accent); }
  .muted { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 400; }
  .duo {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    padding: 0 1.4rem;
  }
  .jur h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }
  .jur .map-box { height: 46vh; min-height: 340px; }
  .jur .outliers { margin-top: 0.6rem; font-size: 0.83rem; }
  .intro {
    padding: 0.4rem 1.4rem 0.2rem;
  }
  .intro p {
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: none;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .intro .scale-note {
    color: var(--brand);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
  .table-card {
    border: 1.5px solid var(--hairline);
    border-radius: 10px;
    background: var(--surface);
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.6rem 0.4rem;
  }
  .table-card .outliers { margin-top: 0; }
  .story {
    padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem 0.6rem;
  }
  .jur-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .jur-head h2 { margin: 0; }
  .search-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-left: auto;
  }
  .search-row input {
    width: clamp(14rem, 22vw, 22rem);
    background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--ink);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.7rem;
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
  }
  .search-row input::placeholder { color: var(--muted); }
  .search-row input:focus {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
    outline-offset: 1px;
  }
  .search-count {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .story h2 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .story ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .story li { margin: 0.45rem 0; color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 1.1rem; }
  .story strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .story p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.82rem; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .duo { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
